# The fifth quantitative component, and the first that is a screen.
#
# Everything before it fits in a row and pays for that by having no numbers on it. A sparkline is
# a shape, and the reader has to already know what it is a shape of - which is the right trade in
# a list, where the row above names the reading and the bar beside it says how far along. It
# stops being the right trade the moment somebody stops to look, and this is that press.
#
# Watch what the room buys. The vertical has its two ends labelled, so "high" is a number rather
# than a feeling. The horizontal says how long, which is the difference between a mesh that got
# busy this afternoon and one that got busy in the last ten minutes. The thresholds the card's
# bar cuts notches at are rules across the plot, so the trend can be *seen* crossing them. And
# there are two lines, which is the thing a row could never have: our own transmit share inside
# the channel's total, named by a legend a row has no space for - and the legend says where each
# of them has got to, which is the one number the shape and the two ends cannot state.

scene demo
delay 240
config

# An hour of mesh in a few seconds. Each `airtime` line is one LocalStats report, which is what
# the radio sends its attached client every few minutes; `stats` goes first so the counter rows
# have the radio's real traffic under them rather than a row of zeroes.
tab status
stats
hold 400
airtime 6 2
hold 400
airtime 9 3
hold 400
airtime 14 4
hold 400
airtime 13 4
hold 400
airtime 21 6
hold 400
airtime 28 9
hold 400
airtime 34 11
hold 400
airtime 47 15
hold 900

# The verb only appears once there are two readings to draw a line between, and it appears on
# the Mesh card, one step down - which is where the *card* is. It used to be appended after the
# Radio card's refresh whatever the column looked like, because the cursor was an index into the
# verbs and a verb arriving anywhere but the end changed what the next A press did. The cursor
# names a verb now, so the list is written in the order the cards draw.
key down
hold 900
key a
hold 2400

# ---- how far back, and how far up ------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The two things the picture decides for itself, and the two the reader can see it deciding.
#
# The vertical does not run to 100% unless something has been near it. The ceiling contracts to a
# rung of a fixed ladder - a hundredth, a fiftieth, a twentieth, a tenth, a quarter, a half, all
# of it - and the top label is that rung, which is the whole of what makes it honest: the lie in
# auto-scaling was never the scale, it was a scale the picture did not state. The floor stays
# where the reading's domain put it, so a mesh at one percent is a shape rather than a flat line
# along the bottom of an empty rectangle, and a fall of two percent is still two percent of
# something.
#
# The horizontal is the reader's. Left and Right walk the strip over the plot - the one screen
# after the map where those two are not the tab switch, and the shoulders staying the tabs is
# what pays for it. The window only ever narrows, so a span wider than the readings leaves the
# caption naming what there turned out to be rather than what was asked for.
key left
hold 1000
key left
hold 1000
key right
hold 1000
key right
hold 1200

# The same picture in every theme. Nothing here is switched by hand: the lines take the series
# palette by position, so line 0 is the same colour on every theme and the legend under the plot
# goes on meaning what it said. The colour-blind theme is where that contract bites - its
# entries are four of Okabe-Ito's eight, chosen on lightness rather than hue, because two lines a
# dichromat can tell apart by hue are still one line in sunlight.
theme light
hold 1300
theme contrast
hold 1300
theme colorblind
hold 1600

# And back out. B is the only way off this screen and the action bar says so. Up, Down and A are
# swallowed rather than reaching the cards underneath - there is no cursor on a chart, and down
# there Down would move one nobody can see and A would run whichever verb it had landed on. Left
# and Right are the exception and the reason is on the screen: they have something here to move.
theme dark
hold 600
key b
hold 1400
